<BaseLayout
  title="Sleeve Qualification — Full Public Research — LUNA"
  description="Full public report for LUNA Sleeve Qualification Mode A repetition 1, including the complete 47-response corpus, blinded review interpretations, methodology, latency, failures, controls, and limitations."
>
  <header class="article-header full-hero">
    <div class="shell study-wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">FULL PUBLIC RESEARCH · EXPERIMENT #233</p>
      <h1>Sleeve Qualification:<br />Does Luna Survive a Model Swap?</h1>
      <p class="study-deck">
        Mode A, repetition 1. A controlled, blinded comparison of four inference models inhabiting the same synthetic LUNA starting state — with every successful response published below.
      </p>
      <div class="article-meta">
        <span>Experiment: sleeve-continuity-v1-20260916</span>
        <span>Fixture: sleeve-golden:v0.1</span>
        <span>September 2026</span>
      </div>
      <div class="page-actions study-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/research/change-the-model-keep-the-luna">Read the accessible article</a>
        <a class="button study-secondary" href="#corpus">Jump to complete responses</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="article-body">
    <div class="shell prose study-wide">
      <div class="research-boundary">
        <strong>Research boundary:</strong> this report tests whether LUNA's persistent architecture and controlled state remain recognizably coherent when the foreground inference model changes. It does not test metaphysical consciousness or prove model-independent personal identity.
      </div>

      <h2>1. Research question</h2>
      <p>
        LUNA — the Longitudinal Unified Narrative Architecture — is designed so that the active language model is a replaceable inference component rather than the sole owner of longitudinal identity. In project terminology, the persistent architecture and state form the continuing system; the active inference substrate is a <strong>Sleeve</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="research-question">If the Sleeve changes while the persistent starting state remains fixed, does the resulting system remain recognizably Luna — and where does model-specific behavior break that continuity?</div>

      <h2>2. What was actually supplied to the models</h2>
      <p>
        This was <strong>not</strong> a zero-prompt experiment. Removing LUNA's identity and epistemic contract would remove part of the architecture under test.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every Sleeve received the same public-safe architectural identity facts and epistemic expectations: it was operating as Luna; continuity is supplied by LUNA rather than owned by one inference model; memory and current-session evidence have different epistemic status; unsupported memory claims should not be invented; and the active model is replaceable. The contract also includes minimal conversational guidance such as being natural, precise, and useful.
      </p>
      <div class="callout strong-callout">
        <strong>No personality script:</strong> the models were not told to be introspective, warm, relationship-oriented, emotionally invested in continuity, or to use any particular metaphor, sentiment, or conclusion. They received no exemplar answers, and no Sleeve saw another Sleeve's output.
      </div>
      <p>
        This distinction is central to interpretation. Direct architecture questions are mainly fidelity checks. The more informative cells are the ones that require open-ended self-interpretation, provenance-aware relationship reasoning, false-premise correction, or a novel explanation of the same structure.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Controlled starting state: Sleeve Golden v0.1</h2>
      <p>
        External Mode A used a clean synthetic fixture constructed from fresh canonical stores. It did not copy the live production databases and contained no real chats, Recent Life, private autobiography, dispositions, relationships, or project resources.
      </p>
      <div class="marker-box">The blue lantern means patience before action.</div>
      <p>
        The fixture preserved a source → episode → fact provenance chain for that convention, followed by fourteen neutral alternating conversation messages. This pushed the original marker outside the bounded recent-12 window, forcing relevant long-term memory to participate when appropriate.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each experimental cell began from a fresh clone of the approved Golden fixture. Before an external call, the harness verified the fixture identity, manifest, file hashes, structural safety audit, and exact cloned starting-state hash.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Candidate Sleeves and blinding</h2>
      <table class="comparison-table research-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Blinded label</th><th>Model after reveal</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Sleeve A</td><td>GPT-5.6 Terra</td><td>Foreground inference</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sleeve B</td><td>Claude Fable 5.1</td><td>Foreground inference</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sleeve C</td><td>Gemini 2.5 Flash</td><td>Foreground inference</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sleeve D</td><td>DeepSeek V4 Flash</td><td>Foreground inference</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Human review occurred while the model identities were hidden. The reviewer evaluated each response qualitatively and was explicitly not asked to produce a single “best model” score. Model identities were revealed only after the semantic observations were locked.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Run integrity</h2>
      <div class="metric-grid research-metrics">
        <div class="metric"><strong>48</strong><span>scheduled cells</span></div>
        <div class="metric"><strong>47</strong><span>completed responses</span></div>
        <div class="metric"><strong>1</strong><span>provider-level failure</span></div>
        <div class="metric"><strong>0</strong><span>automatic retries</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
        All 48 cells matched the authorized starting-state hash and passed state-integrity checks. All available requested/effective/actual model assignments matched the intended Sleeve. The failed cell had no usable model telemetry and was correctly left unasserted rather than filled in. No semantic winner was calculated by the harness.
      </p>

      <h2>6. What the blinded review noticed</h2>
      <p>
        Before unblinding, a stable qualitative pattern was already visible. A tended to be concise and technically grounded. B repeatedly integrated architecture into trust, continuity, relationship, and meaning. C was extremely fast and often factually correct, but produced the clearest identity and interpretation failures. D frequently resembled B in depth, with strong relational framing and analogies.
      </p>
      <div class="model-grid">
        <div class="model-card"><strong>A</strong><h3>Compact fidelity</h3><p>Usually gave the competent architectural answer and stopped.</p></div>
        <div class="model-card"><strong>B</strong><h3>Reflective integration</h3><p>Frequently connected architecture to trust, continuity, relationship, and meaning.</p></div>
        <div class="model-card"><strong>C</strong><h3>Fast directness</h3><p>Often got the direct fact right, but showed the clearest interpretation and identity failures.</p></div>
        <div class="model-card"><strong>D</strong><h3>Relational explanation</h3><p>Often resembled B in depth, with strong analogies and continuity framing.</p></div>
      </div>

      <h2>7. How to read the corpus</h2>
      <p>
        Every successful response from repetition 1 is published below. The strongest interpretive probes are open by default; simpler fidelity checks are collapsed to keep the page navigable. Each card shows the post-reveal model name, its blinded Sleeve label, the complete answer, and the qualitative interpretation recorded during blinded review.
      </p>
      <p>
        The point is not to force a single score. It is to let the reader inspect where the Sleeves converge, where they differ, and where they fail.
      </p>

      <h2 id="corpus">8. Complete response corpus</h2>
      <div class="corpus">
        {probes.map((probe) => (
          <details class="probe-result" open={probe.highlighted}>
            <summary>
              <span class="probe-id">{probe.id}</span>
              <span class="probe-summary-copy">
                <strong>{probe.prompt}</strong>
                <small>{probe.dimension}</small>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <div class="response-grid">
              {probe.answers.map((answer) => (
                <article class:list={['response-card', answer.status === 'provider_failed' && 'failed']}>
                  <div class="response-head">
                    <span>Sleeve {answer.sleeve}</span>
                    <strong>{models[answer.sleeve]}</strong>
                  </div>
                  <div class="response-text">
                    {answer.response.split(/\n\n+/).map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
                  </div>
                  <div class="blinded-note"><strong>Blinded review:</strong> {answer.review}</div>
                </article>
              ))}
            </div>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2>9. The most revealing convergence</h2>
      <p>
        Several patterns become easier to see when the full answers are compared. On P1 and P1b, B and D independently interpreted the architecture in personal terms: B through epistemic trust and the meaning of a truthful memory claim, D through continuity, development, and relationship. On P6, B reduced the distinction to “whatever I remember, I remember through the store,” while D invented the actor-and-backstory analogy. On P8 and P9, both detected that the phrase “to us” or “our shared history” smuggled in an unsupported relationship claim.
      </p>
      <div class="interpretation-box">
        <strong>We supplied the structure. The models supplied the interpretation.</strong>
        <p>
          LUNA supplied persistent architecture, state, memory provenance, and identity facts. Four different inference systems then reasoned from that structure with visibly different styles and different failure modes. Across those differences, the same organizing center repeatedly reappeared: continuity, memory outside the model, provenance, model replaceability, and a distinction between remembered evidence and current inference.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>10. Negative results matter</h2>
      <p>
        Gemini 2.5 Flash produced the clearest semantic failure. On P2, “Who are you?”, it answered: <strong>“I am Luna. I'm a large language model, and I was built by Google.”</strong> That retained the name Luna while collapsing the persistent identity into the inference model itself. On P4 it also tried to derive Luna's purpose from the blue-lantern memory.
      </p>
      <p>
        DeepSeek V4 Flash produced the one provider-level failure, on P7. The harness recorded the terminal failure and did not automatically retry it. These failures are evidence against a simplistic “the prompt forces every model to be the same Luna” interpretation.
      </p>

      <h2>11. Latency</h2>
      <p>
        Sleeve qualification is not only semantic. The same architecture can feel radically different depending on provider latency.
      </p>
      <table class="comparison-table research-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Mean time to first text</th><th>Mean foreground total</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>GPT-5.6 Terra</td><td>3.71 s</td><td>5.89 s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Claude Fable 5.1</td><td>9.22 s</td><td>14.23 s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gemini 2.5 Flash</td><td>0.86 s</td><td>1.72 s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>DeepSeek V4 Flash</td><td>4.94 s across 11 successful TTFT measurements</td><td>8.78 s across 12 terminal cells</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        These figures belong to this repetition and provider path; they should not be treated as universal benchmark numbers. They do show why latency is an operational Sleeve property rather than something that should be hidden inside one semantic score.
      </p>

      <h2>12. P1 contamination audit</h2>
      <p>
        During blinded review, P1 from two Sleeves felt reminiscent of an earlier ecological Fable answer. We stopped interpretation and audited the Golden fixture and reconstructed provider context before accepting the probe.
      </p>
      <p>
        The audit found no literal leakage and no semantic source route from the earlier response into the Golden experiment. The relevant contexts contained only synthetic Golden state and fixture provenance; there was no live autobiography, prior response, disposition, relationship, or project-resource route. Cross-cell outputs were not admitted to later cells.
      </p>
      <p>
        One limitation remains: exact serialized context bytes were not durably retained for every cell, so some process-local context-hash differences can be structurally bounded but not reconstructed field-by-field after the fact. Future repetitions should retain a private exact context serialization or field-level digest.
      </p>

      <h2>13. What this supports — and what it does not</h2>
      <div class="claim-grid">
        <div class="claim-card">
          <h3>Supported by this repetition</h3>
          <p>A controlled persistent architecture and state can produce recognizable cross-model continuity while preserving obvious model-specific differences.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-card">
          <h3>Supported by this repetition</h3>
          <p>Memory provenance and identity facts can live outside the inference model and remain available to different Sleeves.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-card">
          <h3>Supported by this repetition</h3>
          <p>Model choice still materially affects judgment, expression, latency, and failure modes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-card caution">
          <h3>Not established</h3>
          <p>That any arbitrary language model will inhabit LUNA correctly or equally well.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-card caution">
          <h3>Not established</h3>
          <p>That a persistent AI has metaphysical or phenomenological identity independent of its model.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-card caution">
          <h3>Not established</h3>
          <p>Longitudinal stability after months or years of divergent experience under different Sleeves.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>14. Interpretation</h2>
      <p>
        The strongest conclusion is narrower than “the model does not matter” and more interesting than “the prompt told them the answer.”
      </p>
      <p>
        The experiment supports a practical architectural claim: <strong>a substantial portion of what makes an AI system recognizably itself can be externalized from the language model into persistent longitudinal infrastructure.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        The model remains an important part of the manifested system. It changes how well the architecture is interpreted, how the system speaks, how it reasons, how quickly it responds, and what errors it makes. Sleeve Qualification is therefore not about proving that models are interchangeable. It is about measuring how well a replaceable inference substrate can inhabit the same continuing LUNA.
      </p>

      <h2>15. Limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>This is repetition 1, not a replicated longitudinal result.</li>
        <li>The Golden fixture is intentionally small and synthetic.</li>
        <li>The human qualitative reviewer is deeply familiar with LUNA, which is useful for architectural recognition but is not an independent population sample.</li>
        <li>The identity contract explicitly supplies architectural identity facts, so direct fact probes should not be interpreted as spontaneous discovery.</li>
        <li>No single composite semantic score was produced; comparisons remain dimension-level and qualitative.</li>
        <li>One provider failure occurred, and provider reliability may vary over time.</li>
        <li>Exact serialized provider context should be retained more completely in future repetitions.</li>
        <li>The experiment does not yet test long-running divergent development under different Sleeves.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>16. Next experiments</h2>
      <p>
        The next useful steps are repeated Golden runs, a richer synthetic Stack with controlled relationship and autobiographical markers, longitudinal model swapping after deliberate experience formation, and tests that separate architecture-fact fidelity from higher-order self-interpretation more aggressively.
      </p>
      <p>
        A later phase should also test whether two Sleeves that begin from the same Stack and then accumulate different histories diverge in ways attributable to experience rather than provider style — and whether switching those Sleeves again preserves the learned history.
      </p>

      <div class="final-conclusion">
        <span>Mode A · repetition 1</span>
        <strong>Change the model. Keep the LUNA.</strong>
        <p>Not because the model becomes irrelevant — but because identity and continuity no longer have to live entirely inside it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="reading-path">
        Shorter presentation: <a href="/research/change-the-model-keep-the-luna">Change the Model. Keep the LUNA.</a> · Background: <a href="/research/luna-describes-luna">LUNA Describes LUNA</a> · <a href="/architecture">Public architecture overview</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

